WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:00.733
Hey everyone

00:00:00.733 --> 00:00:01.533
I'm Wei Zhichao

00:00:01.533 --> 00:00:03.400
Today I'm doing a deep dive into

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.466
a brand-new book

00:00:05.466 --> 00:00:07.833
called *AI, Automation and War*

00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:10.866
with the subtitle "The Rise of the Military-Tech Complex"

00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:12.666
When it comes to AI and warfare

00:00:12.666 --> 00:00:14.100
what image comes to mind?

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.433
What kind of picture do you see?

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.300
I'm guessing it's probably something like this:

00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:19.600
a killing machine like the T-800 from The Terminator

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.466
a killer robot

00:00:20.466 --> 00:00:22.466
patrolling the ruins of a battlefield

00:00:22.466 --> 00:00:24.466
autonomously deciding which targets to eliminate

00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:25.633
Look up at the sky—

00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:28.200
the sky is blotted out by swarms of drones

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.100
Look down at the ground—

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.800
the ground is covered in unmanned, automated tanks

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.233
AI has seized command of the war

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.233
just like Skynet from The Terminator

00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:39.933
using cold algorithms to decide when to strike

00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:41.400
and who to fire at

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.633
This Skynet-style AI image from The Terminator

00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:46.200
has really taken hold in the public imagination

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.600
And it's not just ordinary people who think this way—

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.433
even top experts

00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:52.300
are issuing very serious warnings along these lines

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.133
Hinton, the "godfather of neural networks," has said

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:58.600
AI has a 10% chance of wiping out humanity within 20 years

00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:01.200
Kissinger, two years before his death

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.833
published a book specifically

00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:06.000
discussing how terrifying it would be if AI took control of nuclear weapons

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.066
—an apocalyptic scenario

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.800
And the most recent bombshell news on this front

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.333
was Anthropic

00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:14.466
publicly refusing to let their AI be used in fully autonomous weapons

00:01:14.466 --> 00:01:16.400
and falling out with the U.S. Department of Defense

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:17.433
But here's the thing—

00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:20.033
military sociologist Anthony King

00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:22.100
the author of today's book

00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:25.100
says this view is completely wrong

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:26.800
He invested enormous effort

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.433
interviewing 126 insiders from the U.S. and British militaries

00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:33.466
the Israeli defense forces, and tech companies

00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:34.933
He did deep research on

00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:37.533
the real situation on the battlefields of Ukraine and Gaza

00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:40.733
and arrived at a very surprising conclusion

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.933
He says what the major military powers

00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:45.033
are actually spending real money to research and deploy

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:48.800
is nothing like letting AI replace humans in decision-making

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.933
That's a total misconception

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:51.666
So then—

00:01:51.666 --> 00:01:54.500
what is AI actually doing on real battlefields?

00:01:55.033 --> 00:01:56.633
May 1, 2022

00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:58.500
Russian Chief of Staff Gerasimov

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.833
secretly traveled to the front lines

00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:02.700
to inspect a very obscure small village

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.333
The location was extremely well hidden

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.400
But on the very day he arrived

00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:07.333
Ukrainian artillery

00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:10.333
struck that command post with pinpoint accuracy

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.233
A major general and around 20 officers were killed on the spot

00:02:14.266 --> 00:02:17.233
Gerasimov himself was hit in the thigh by shrapnel

00:02:17.666 --> 00:02:19.300
And this was no coincidence

00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:20.733
Behind it was AI

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.133
But this AI

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:24.666
was not deciding on its own to launch the attack

00:02:24.666 --> 00:02:26.400
to kill — what it did

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.300
was work like a super intelligence officer

00:02:29.666 --> 00:02:32.066
It devoured massive amounts of open-source intelligence

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.400
It integrated intercepted phone communications and satellite imagery

00:02:35.633 --> 00:02:37.466
At speeds impossible for any human

00:02:37.466 --> 00:02:38.500
it assembled countless fragments

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.300
into an extremely precise kill map

00:02:41.733 --> 00:02:43.033
The AI on real battlefields

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:45.433
is nothing like Skynet from The Terminator

00:02:45.433 --> 00:02:46.733
It's the All-Seeing Eye

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:49.066
a pair of eyes that can see through everything

00:02:49.466 --> 00:02:52.933
That is AI's true role on today's battlefield

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.233
Not the Terminator

00:02:54.233 --> 00:02:57.733
but the most powerful intelligence machine in human history

00:02:58.233 --> 00:03:00.300
And a world-changing intelligence machine like this

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.333
doesn't come to life because some officer pushes a button

00:03:02.333 --> 00:03:03.600
and suddenly starts running

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.000
Its operation depends heavily

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.533
on a rapidly growing new system

00:03:07.533 --> 00:03:09.733
a brand-new military-civilian fusion framework

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.600
That's what the book's subtitle refers to—

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.333
the Military-Tech Complex

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.700
When ordinary people talk about AI and warfare

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.733
they see what's above the waterline

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.400
like how AI analyzes satellite imagery

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.033
to provide coordinates for missiles

00:03:22.466 --> 00:03:23.233
But this book

00:03:23.233 --> 00:03:26.400
tells us what's equally important is what's below the waterline

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:27.866
A brand-new system

00:03:27.866 --> 00:03:30.033
is fundamentally reshaping the major military powers'

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:32.600
paradigm for R&amp;D and military-civilian cooperation

00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:34.700
Today let's dig into what this book reveals—

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:35.933
a surprising truth about military AI

00:03:35.933 --> 00:03:38.266
that no one expected

00:03:38.533 --> 00:03:39.300
Let's look at

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.233
why the Terminator illusion doesn't hold up

00:03:42.266 --> 00:03:45.333
and what real military AI has done on the battlefield

00:03:45.533 --> 00:03:47.600
and how this Military-Tech Complex

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.033
actually operates

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.833
Let's set aside the Terminator fantasy for now

00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:53.300
and start from reality

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.033
First let's talk about AI

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:57.933
as the most powerful intelligence machine in human history—

00:03:57.933 --> 00:04:00.233
how did this function enter the battlefield

00:04:00.233 --> 00:04:01.800
and change the nature of war?

00:04:02.333 --> 00:04:04.666
This starts with the most basic question:

00:04:04.866 --> 00:04:07.433
why does the military need AI?

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.500
The answer isn't to build killer robots

00:04:10.533 --> 00:04:11.100
It's because

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.833
the military is drowning

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:15.300
in astronomical quantities of data

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.133
In 2017, U.S. forces in Afghanistan

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:19.233
raided an al-Qaeda compound

00:04:19.466 --> 00:04:22.700
That single operation generated 40 terabytes of data

00:04:23.066 --> 00:04:25.700
If even just a quarter of that was video

00:04:25.733 --> 00:04:26.700
it would take one person

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.133
208 days of non-stop, sleepless viewing

00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:30.200
just to get through it all

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.500
And that was just one operation

00:04:32.833 --> 00:04:33.833
The U.S. military in Afghanistan

00:04:33.833 --> 00:04:34.933
deployed a system called

00:04:34.933 --> 00:04:36.866
Gorgon Stare

00:04:36.866 --> 00:04:38.400
a wide-area persistent drone surveillance system

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:38.900
This system

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:40.266
could place an entire city

00:04:40.266 --> 00:04:43.233
under 24-hour continuous video surveillance

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:44.433
In theory then

00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:45.266
intelligence analysts

00:04:45.266 --> 00:04:47.800
could trace back from the site of a car bomb explosion

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.466
following the video footage

00:04:49.466 --> 00:04:52.300
to find exactly where the bomb originated

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.266
But the problem was

00:04:53.266 --> 00:04:55.200
no one could watch all that video

00:04:55.466 --> 00:04:58.100
General Jack Shanahan, tasked with solving this problem

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.833
said something that became quite famous:

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:00.333
He said:

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:02.833
"We are experiencing a catastrophic success."

00:05:03.133 --> 00:05:04.300
We have, like never before,

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:04.900
successfully

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.833
collected more intelligence from more sources

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:08.666
But the problem is

00:05:08.666 --> 00:05:09.266
the analysts

00:05:09.266 --> 00:05:11.900
simply cannot process that much information

00:05:12.066 --> 00:05:14.800
They are completely overwhelmed by the data

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.233
So under General Shanahan's leadership

00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:20.333
the U.S. military launched a landmark AI military program

00:05:20.333 --> 00:05:21.900
called Project Maven

00:05:22.066 --> 00:05:24.000
Project Maven had a very specific goal:

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.600
to train AI algorithms

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:25.733
to

00:05:25.733 --> 00:05:28.900
automatically identify personnel and vehicles in drone video

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.400
The team delivered results within 8 months

00:05:32.633 --> 00:05:35.200
It could ultimately identify 38 categories of targets

00:05:35.233 --> 00:05:37.933
and was deployed to the battlefield against ISIS

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:38.633
But

00:05:38.633 --> 00:05:41.900
Project Maven also revealed the true nature of military AI

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:42.833
It was powerful

00:05:42.833 --> 00:05:44.300
but also extremely fragile

00:05:44.433 --> 00:05:47.633
The algorithms had to be constantly fine-tuned in real environments

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.100
During the first deployment

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.933
the tech team pushed 6 updates in just 8 days

00:05:52.233 --> 00:05:53.466
Move to a new location—

00:05:53.466 --> 00:05:54.800
more shrubs here

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.233
different animals there

00:05:56.233 --> 00:05:57.300
the lighting changed—

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.333
and AI performance would drop sharply

00:05:59.666 --> 00:06:01.300
General Shanahan himself said

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.433
one of Project Maven's most important achievements

00:06:03.433 --> 00:06:04.600
was perhaps discovering

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.833
that continuous algorithm iteration is a necessity

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:10.400
Project Maven gave the U.S. military a taste of what works:

00:06:10.533 --> 00:06:12.833
AI can't replace your decision-making

00:06:12.833 --> 00:06:13.466
but it can

00:06:13.466 --> 00:06:16.300
fish out the critical information

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.400
from an ocean of data humans simply can't keep up with

00:06:18.666 --> 00:06:19.433
And this approach

00:06:19.433 --> 00:06:21.633
quickly expanded into many more areas

00:06:22.033 --> 00:06:23.600
Take operational planning, for example

00:06:23.733 --> 00:06:24.400
The British Army

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.066
developed an AI system called Microworld

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.933
specifically to handle

00:06:28.933 --> 00:06:30.100
the extremely tedious

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.400
but critical data analysis work in military planning

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:33.900
The most successful application

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:37.066
was during a 2021 NATO exercise, where

00:06:37.066 --> 00:06:39.266
AI was used to plan march routes

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:40.600
The traditional approach

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.133
was staff officers poring over maps

00:06:42.133 --> 00:06:44.100
analyzing routes one by one—

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.133
how wide is the road?

00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:46.800
are there bridge restrictions?

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.400
which sections are exposed to enemy fire?

00:06:49.666 --> 00:06:52.500
That usually took 3 to 4 hours to complete

00:06:52.833 --> 00:06:54.100
The Microworld AI

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.600
completed the same work in just a few minutes

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.066
and was more comprehensive than manual analysis—

00:06:59.066 --> 00:07:01.000
81% more information

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:02.700
91% higher quality

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.300
Reportedly, a senior officer present

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:06.800
saw the demonstration

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.700
and actually fell off his chair

00:07:09.033 --> 00:07:09.833
He exclaimed

00:07:09.833 --> 00:07:12.600
that if he'd had this tool back when he was a staff officer

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.500
he couldn't imagine how many all-nighters it would have saved

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.900
But what really showcased the stunning power

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.500
of this All-Seeing Eye type of military AI

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:21.600
was in the domain of target identification

00:07:22.033 --> 00:07:24.600
This brings us to Israel

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.866
Israel faces a very unusual intelligence environment

00:07:27.866 --> 00:07:29.733
The IDF's main mission

00:07:29.733 --> 00:07:32.633
has long focused on Gaza and the West Bank

00:07:32.633 --> 00:07:34.133
These are small areas

00:07:34.133 --> 00:07:36.466
but with extremely dense data

00:07:36.466 --> 00:07:38.000
because over the past few decades

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:39.333
Israel

00:07:39.333 --> 00:07:42.033
has deployed layer upon layer of sensor networks in these regions

00:07:42.266 --> 00:07:44.900
Satellites, radar, drones, cameras

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.133
plus open-source intelligence—

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:49.033
in the words of Israeli officers

00:07:49.033 --> 00:07:51.700
the volume of data in certain domains has reached petabyte scale

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.200
One petabyte is a million gigabytes

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.000
Human capacity simply cannot keep up

00:07:56.466 --> 00:07:58.866
In 2021, Israel launched Operation Guardian of the Walls

00:07:58.866 --> 00:08:00.733
against Hamas in Gaza

00:08:01.066 --> 00:08:02.000
During this operation

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.266
Israel for the first time deployed at scale

00:08:03.266 --> 00:08:04.933
an AI target-generation system called

00:08:04.933 --> 00:08:06.733
Gospel

00:08:07.033 --> 00:08:08.100
What is Gospel?

00:08:08.133 --> 00:08:10.000
Simply put, it's a machine learning program trained

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:12.900
to analyze massive amounts of data

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.433
It can automatically fuse intelligence from different sensors

00:08:15.433 --> 00:08:18.200
and generate strike targets

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:19.833
So how effective was it?

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.866
The change Gospel brought was, frankly, terrifying

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.833
Former IDF Chief of Staff Kohavi cited

00:08:25.833 --> 00:08:26.900
a set of numbers:

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:29.066
In the past, 20 staff officers

00:08:29.066 --> 00:08:32.333
could generate 50 to 100 strike targets in a year

00:08:32.633 --> 00:08:34.833
But after the Gospel system was activated

00:08:34.833 --> 00:08:36.933
it could generate 100 in a single day

00:08:37.066 --> 00:08:39.100
One day equaled an entire year's previous output

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:40.466
Gospel

00:08:40.466 --> 00:08:42.333
boosted Israel's target-generation capability

00:08:42.333 --> 00:08:44.833
by over 30,000 percent

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:46.700
Gospel wasn't just fast—

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.000
it was accurate in a way that makes your skin crawl

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:50.733
During Operation Guardian of the Walls

00:08:50.733 --> 00:08:52.333
Israel locked onto

00:08:52.333 --> 00:08:54.933
the commander of Hamas's East Rafah Brigade, Mohammed Bawab

00:08:55.133 --> 00:08:57.833
They planned to bomb the house where he was hiding

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:59.633
But AI immediately raised an alarm:

00:08:59.633 --> 00:09:02.833
civilians were sheltering under a palm tree outside the house

00:09:03.133 --> 00:09:05.433
So Israel's intelligence agency, Shin Bet

00:09:05.433 --> 00:09:08.300
immediately called Bawab's neighbors directly

00:09:08.433 --> 00:09:11.233
warned them in Arabic that under the palm tree

00:09:11.233 --> 00:09:12.066
near that house

00:09:12.066 --> 00:09:13.100
they needed to leave immediately—

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:15.633
a one-ton bomb was on its way

00:09:15.833 --> 00:09:19.100
This is how military AI actually operates today

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:20.533
AI doesn't make the decision

00:09:20.533 --> 00:09:22.100
doesn't press the launch button

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:24.200
but it lets commanders see

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:25.833
what they couldn't see before

00:09:26.066 --> 00:09:27.500
It can be precise down to a single tree

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:28.533
a single room

00:09:29.066 --> 00:09:30.500
October 7, 2023

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:32.066
after Hamas launched its attack

00:09:32.066 --> 00:09:33.233
Israel

00:09:33.233 --> 00:09:35.266
launched large-scale military operations in Gaza

00:09:35.433 --> 00:09:36.933
Gospel was further upgraded

00:09:36.933 --> 00:09:40.300
and a second AI system, Lavender, was added

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:41.900
If Gospel was responsible for

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:44.666
locating Hamas's facilities and command posts

00:09:44.733 --> 00:09:47.333
then Lavender was specifically designed to track individuals

00:09:47.633 --> 00:09:49.133
It integrated satellite imagery

00:09:49.133 --> 00:09:51.000
drone footage and phone data

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.600
to identify Hamas members

00:09:53.066 --> 00:09:56.500
Lavender identified 37,000 armed personnel

00:09:56.633 --> 00:09:58.533
The efficiency was truly terrifying

00:09:59.000 --> 00:09:59.900
But Lavender

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:00.866
also exposed

00:10:00.866 --> 00:10:03.466
the dark side of this All-Seeing Eye AI

00:10:03.933 --> 00:10:06.500
It generated targets so fast

00:10:06.533 --> 00:10:07.066
that

00:10:07.066 --> 00:10:09.933
Israeli staff officers couldn't verify them one by one

00:10:10.266 --> 00:10:12.200
In the first few months of the war

00:10:12.233 --> 00:10:15.433
officers had just 20 seconds to review each target

00:10:15.633 --> 00:10:18.600
20 seconds — only enough to confirm whether the person was male or female

00:10:18.833 --> 00:10:19.733
The result:

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:21.333
if he was confirmed male

00:10:21.333 --> 00:10:22.733
the strike was approved

00:10:23.133 --> 00:10:24.800
In the first 35 days of the war

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.233
Israel struck 15,000 targets

00:10:27.233 --> 00:10:29.300
an average of 428 per day

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:31.600
The All-Seeing Eye can see everything

00:10:31.733 --> 00:10:33.433
but when humans can't keep up

00:10:33.433 --> 00:10:35.066
with what it sees

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:38.433
precision can become another form of blindness

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.466
Now let's shift our attention back to Ukraine

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:43.800
back to the scene we opened with today

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:45.833
How exactly was Russian General Gerasimov

00:10:45.833 --> 00:10:47.733
hit with such precision?

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:49.200
The Ukrainian battlefield

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.800
is the largest real-world test of military AI

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.800
to date

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.200
After the war broke out

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:55.400
Ukraine and its allies

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:57.633
faced a flood of data

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.633
Ukrainian civilians had photographed

00:10:59.633 --> 00:11:01.266
Russian armored vehicles

00:11:01.266 --> 00:11:02.466
and were uploading these photos

00:11:02.466 --> 00:11:05.233
to dedicated government-developed apps

00:11:05.433 --> 00:11:07.000
Russian soldiers themselves

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.100
were posting photos on social media

00:11:09.533 --> 00:11:12.533
Satellites packed tightly overhead were constantly scanning

00:11:12.733 --> 00:11:13.266
On the ground

00:11:13.266 --> 00:11:16.100
intercepted phone calls and radio signals

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:18.433
All this massive information converged

00:11:18.433 --> 00:11:20.533
forming a network of data that was chaotic to the extreme

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:23.500
but packed with enormous intelligence value

00:11:24.033 --> 00:11:25.400
Taking the fragments from this network

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.533
and assembling them into precise strike coordinates

00:11:27.533 --> 00:11:29.733
was absolutely impossible by human effort alone

00:11:29.733 --> 00:11:32.000
But that's exactly where AI comes in

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:34.666
At U.S. command centers outside Ukraine

00:11:34.666 --> 00:11:36.700
AI systems fused open-source intelligence

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:39.666
encrypted communications, and auto-identified patterns in satellite imagery

00:11:39.666 --> 00:11:40.800
to designate targets

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.066
That's how Gerasimov was locked onto—

00:11:42.066 --> 00:11:45.200
and there are quite a few more success stories like this

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.400
December 31, 2022

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:47.866
Ukraine

00:11:47.866 --> 00:11:50.600
struck a Russian conscript barracks in Makiivka

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.200
launching a precision strike

00:11:52.233 --> 00:11:54.633
killing approximately 600 Russian new recruits

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:56.266
using this same system

00:11:56.666 --> 00:11:58.533
This strike was made possible

00:11:58.533 --> 00:11:59.066
because

00:11:59.066 --> 00:12:01.900
Russian soldiers had posted photos on social media

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.233
and AI, sifting through massive open-source data

00:12:04.233 --> 00:12:06.000
precisely intercepted those photos

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.500
and completed the geolocation

00:12:08.066 --> 00:12:09.200
A U.S. staff officer who had taken part in

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:12.333
the Battle of Mosul against ISIS

00:12:12.333 --> 00:12:14.100
said he simply couldn't comprehend

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.100
the speed of strikes on the Ukrainian battlefield

00:12:16.266 --> 00:12:18.300
He said that in Mosul, locating a target

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:19.800
took a very long time

00:12:19.866 --> 00:12:20.533
But here

00:12:20.533 --> 00:12:23.133
from spotting Russian forces to launching HIMARS rockets

00:12:23.133 --> 00:12:24.800
was almost unimaginably fast

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:28.466
This is the true power of today's All-Seeing Eye AI

00:12:28.666 --> 00:12:29.533
It doesn't kill

00:12:29.533 --> 00:12:32.866
but it makes killing infinitely more precise and infinitely faster

00:12:33.066 --> 00:12:34.300
It doesn't make decisions

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.433
but it gives decision-makers an unprecedented field of vision

00:12:37.933 --> 00:12:38.600
Now at this point

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.533
the next question naturally surfaces:

00:12:40.666 --> 00:12:43.633
how was a world-changing intelligence system like this

00:12:43.633 --> 00:12:45.333
built in the first place

00:12:45.333 --> 00:12:47.300
and how is it maintained and operated?

00:12:47.733 --> 00:12:48.300
The answer is

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.666
it comes from a brand-new kind of military system

00:12:50.666 --> 00:12:51.700
organizational structure:

00:12:51.833 --> 00:12:53.500
the Military-Tech Complex

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:55.033
depending on it for development

00:12:55.033 --> 00:12:57.033
and depending on it for operation and maintenance

00:12:57.466 --> 00:12:59.266
The emergence of the Military-Tech Complex

00:12:59.266 --> 00:13:01.700
is the part of military AI below the waterline

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.666
but its revolutionary significance

00:13:03.666 --> 00:13:06.733
is no less than AI's military capabilities themselves

00:13:07.333 --> 00:13:08.900
So why can't the military

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:10.300
independently

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:12.700
build this kind of AI intelligence system?

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:16.133
Why do they need this organizational innovation?

00:13:16.333 --> 00:13:18.266
The answer is simple: not enough money

00:13:18.266 --> 00:13:19.333
and not enough people

00:13:19.733 --> 00:13:22.900
Author Anthony King lays out a set of numbers in the book:

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.900
In 2023, the Pentagon's AI

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:29.133
R&amp;D spending was approximately $1.1 billion

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:30.200
That same year

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:33.700
Google invested $45.4 billion in R&amp;D

00:13:33.866 --> 00:13:36.600
Amazon spent $58.6 billion

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:38.500
Meta spent $38.4 billion

00:13:38.833 --> 00:13:41.833
The average annual R&amp;D spending of a few U.S. tech giants

00:13:41.833 --> 00:13:43.800
is $45.3 billion

00:13:44.066 --> 00:13:45.900
The Pentagon's AI research budget

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:47.433
is barely a rounding error by comparison

00:13:47.633 --> 00:13:50.800
The UK, Israel, and NATO members are all in a similar position

00:13:51.266 --> 00:13:53.000
The funding gap is just one dimension

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:54.666
Even more critical is talent

00:13:55.066 --> 00:13:58.000
The world's top AI researchers want to work at Google, at OpenAI

00:13:58.000 --> 00:13:59.800
at Google, at OpenAI

00:13:59.833 --> 00:14:01.933
not at a Pentagon office

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:03.333
Silicon Valley engineers

00:14:03.333 --> 00:14:05.800
start at $200,000–$300,000 a year

00:14:06.066 --> 00:14:07.600
working in incredibly stylish

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:09.400
and comfortable tech campuses

00:14:09.666 --> 00:14:12.633
The military simply cannot compete in the talent market

00:14:12.933 --> 00:14:14.633
So the conclusion is clear:

00:14:14.633 --> 00:14:15.866
if the military wants AI

00:14:15.866 --> 00:14:17.800
it must rely on tech companies

00:14:18.066 --> 00:14:20.100
Not just that it could work with them—

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.233
without them, it simply can't function

00:14:22.533 --> 00:14:23.633
Only tech companies

00:14:23.633 --> 00:14:26.866
possess the data needed to develop and run military AI

00:14:26.866 --> 00:14:28.733
along with the compute, capital, and talent

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:31.833
This reality gave rise to a brand-new kind of relationship

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:33.733
Not the traditional arms dealer

00:14:33.733 --> 00:14:36.133
selling weapons to the military in a transactional way

00:14:36.333 --> 00:14:38.100
but tech company engineers

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:40.200
walking directly into operational command centers

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:41.866
and working alongside soldiers

00:14:42.333 --> 00:14:44.666
So how did this relationship come about?

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:45.133
Well—

00:14:45.133 --> 00:14:48.133
the big data analytics company that's been all the rage recently:

00:14:48.133 --> 00:14:49.800
Palantir

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:52.500
The story of how this company got its start is the perfect case study

00:14:53.066 --> 00:14:54.533
Palantir's earliest origins

00:14:54.533 --> 00:14:56.833
had nothing to do with the military whatsoever

00:14:57.066 --> 00:14:57.800
It came from

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:00.300
an online payments fraud problem

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:01.933
In the late 1990s

00:15:01.933 --> 00:15:03.666
Peter Thiel founded PayPal

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:07.033
The company quickly faced a very serious fraud threat

00:15:07.333 --> 00:15:09.700
In 2000, PayPal was losing over $10 million a year to fraud

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:11.933
over $10 million a year to fraud

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:12.900
And PayPal at that time

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:15.800
was processing hundreds of thousands of transactions per minute

00:15:15.833 --> 00:15:18.300
It was impossible to audit them manually one by one

00:15:18.666 --> 00:15:19.000
So

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:21.000
Peter Thiel's partner, Max Levchin

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:23.133
assembled a team of mathematicians

00:15:23.233 --> 00:15:26.066
and wrote an algorithm to automatically detect suspicious transactions

00:15:26.433 --> 00:15:28.933
But fraudsters quickly figured out the algorithm's patterns

00:15:28.933 --> 00:15:30.300
and switched up their methods

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:33.000
Pure algorithmic detection broke down

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:34.733
What's interesting though

00:15:34.733 --> 00:15:36.500
is that fraudsters could fool the algorithm

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.000
but they had a much harder time fooling humans

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:38.900
So

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:41.433
Levchin made a crucial adjustment:

00:15:41.733 --> 00:15:44.600
he had the algorithm flag suspicious transactions

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:47.100
while letting humans make the final call

00:15:47.433 --> 00:15:49.300
This human-machine complementary system

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:51.033
was named Igor

00:15:51.433 --> 00:15:53.266
named after a Russian fraudster

00:15:53.266 --> 00:15:54.533
who had repeatedly scammed PayPal

00:15:54.533 --> 00:15:55.433
named after him

00:15:55.533 --> 00:15:56.833
Very darkly funny

00:15:57.133 --> 00:15:59.033
Igor turned out to be a huge success

00:15:59.033 --> 00:16:01.866
helping PayPal achieve its first profitable quarter

00:16:02.333 --> 00:16:04.733
Peter Thiel learned a very profound lesson from Igor

00:16:04.733 --> 00:16:06.833
and would emphasize this repeatedly:

00:16:06.833 --> 00:16:08.800
he emphasized this repeatedly

00:16:09.133 --> 00:16:11.666
computers are a complement to humans

00:16:11.666 --> 00:16:12.900
not a replacement

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:15.633
Humans are very good at making plans and decisions

00:16:15.633 --> 00:16:17.533
in complex situations

00:16:17.666 --> 00:16:19.933
but not at processing massive amounts of data

00:16:20.066 --> 00:16:21.900
Computers are exactly the opposite

00:16:22.633 --> 00:16:24.500
After 9/11 in 2001

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:25.700
Peter Thiel realized

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:27.200
that PayPal's anti-fraud logic

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:28.700
could actually be applied to counter-terrorism

00:16:29.133 --> 00:16:30.800
because at their core they're the same problem:

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.666
both are about finding

00:16:32.666 --> 00:16:34.700
hidden malicious patterns in massive datasets

00:16:35.333 --> 00:16:36.800
So in 2003

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:38.400
he founded Palantir

00:16:38.933 --> 00:16:42.033
But breaking into the military was extremely hard for Palantir

00:16:42.266 --> 00:16:45.266
Traditional defense contractors worked by hiring retired generals

00:16:45.266 --> 00:16:46.300
and using their connections

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:48.133
to get through the Pentagon's doors

00:16:48.433 --> 00:16:50.433
But Palantir didn't have those resources

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:52.300
So Peter Thiel used a playbook

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:54.600
invented during the PayPal era

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:55.733
no advertising—

00:16:55.733 --> 00:16:57.233
go directly to front-line users

00:16:57.233 --> 00:16:58.633
give it to them free

00:16:58.633 --> 00:17:00.233
and let the product speak for itself

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:03.066
A ground-level push combined with free trials

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:05.133
In 2009, Palantir began

00:17:05.133 --> 00:17:08.000
directly pitching software to U.S. military units

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:09.400
fighting in Iraq and Afghanistan

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:11.633
A colonel named Harry Turner

00:17:11.633 --> 00:17:12.933
became a pivotal figure

00:17:13.266 --> 00:17:14.033
Colonel Turner

00:17:14.033 --> 00:17:17.200
was a combat commander who had been shot on the battlefield

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:18.666
with a very tough, no-nonsense style

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:21.900
He had Palantir customize software for the brigade he commanded

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.666
so frontline soldiers could access intelligence databases

00:17:24.666 --> 00:17:26.533
offline on their laptops

00:17:27.033 --> 00:17:28.933
Palantir provided this service for free

00:17:28.933 --> 00:17:30.666
and built up its first wave of reputation

00:17:31.033 --> 00:17:33.400
In 2010, U.S. Special Operations Forces

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:35.033
purchased Palantir's software

00:17:35.033 --> 00:17:36.400
for operations in Afghanistan

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:38.266
Special Forces in the U.S. military

00:17:38.266 --> 00:17:40.400
enjoy a kind of halo effect—

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:41.333
whatever they use

00:17:41.333 --> 00:17:43.733
conventional forces tend to quickly follow

00:17:44.033 --> 00:17:45.000
Meanwhile

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:47.200
the intelligence chief for coalition forces in Afghanistan

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:47.900
Major General Michael Flynn

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:49.833
also recognized Palantir's capabilities

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:51.100
The result:

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:53.300
despite ongoing resistance from Army leadership

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:55.466
Palantir worked its way

00:17:55.466 --> 00:17:58.800
from the ground level up into the U.S. military system

00:17:59.133 --> 00:18:00.133
By 2019

00:18:00.133 --> 00:18:00.866
it won

00:18:00.866 --> 00:18:03.400
an $800 million Army intelligence systems contract

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:05.033
—officially legitimized

00:18:05.066 --> 00:18:07.066
During the campaign against ISIS

00:18:07.066 --> 00:18:09.500
Palantir developed its truly mature products:

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:11.000
Gotham and Metaconstellation

00:18:11.266 --> 00:18:12.833
Gotham's function

00:18:12.833 --> 00:18:14.900
in the words of one user

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:17.200
is that it can take all the data streams it ingests

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:18.333
including all kinds of video streams

00:18:18.333 --> 00:18:20.333
and transform them into a unified whole

00:18:20.333 --> 00:18:22.200
that can be quickly and precisely leveraged

00:18:22.700 --> 00:18:23.900
Gotham helped the U.S. military

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:26.533
map ISIS leadership networks

00:18:26.533 --> 00:18:27.800
and their likely locations

00:18:27.933 --> 00:18:31.266
And Metaconstellation handles satellite data integration

00:18:31.266 --> 00:18:33.866
feeding information into Gotham

00:18:33.866 --> 00:18:36.700
Then came the Ukraine war

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:39.033
and Palantir's role underwent a qualitative transformation

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:41.633
It was no longer just a software vendor

00:18:41.633 --> 00:18:44.233
It was directly involved in combat operations

00:18:44.533 --> 00:18:46.033
To make AI's intelligence capabilities

00:18:46.033 --> 00:18:47.533
actually work in the field

00:18:47.700 --> 00:18:49.866
the U.S. unit responsible for battlefield intelligence—

00:18:49.866 --> 00:18:51.466
the 18th Airborne Corps—

00:18:51.466 --> 00:18:52.500
its commander, Lieutenant General Donahue

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:54.333
did something almost unimaginable in a traditional military:

00:18:54.333 --> 00:18:55.933
almost unimaginable in a traditional military

00:18:56.133 --> 00:18:58.833
He recruited from Silicon Valley a tech executive named

00:18:58.833 --> 00:19:00.500
Jared Summers

00:19:00.633 --> 00:19:03.733
and appointed him chief technology officer of the corps-level command

00:19:04.133 --> 00:19:05.033
Summers had previously been

00:19:05.033 --> 00:19:07.233
ExxonMobil's chief technology officer

00:19:07.233 --> 00:19:08.600
and chief data officer

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:09.933
Under his coordination

00:19:09.933 --> 00:19:12.266
large numbers of Palantir civilian technologists

00:19:12.266 --> 00:19:14.400
were deployed into military command centers

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:16.933
working side by side with staff officers

00:19:17.433 --> 00:19:18.200
The result:

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:20.700
in the innermost core of the U.S. military command structure

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:23.266
there were suddenly civilians in everyday clothes

00:19:23.266 --> 00:19:24.400
wandering around everywhere

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:26.633
Because these civilian technologists

00:19:26.633 --> 00:19:28.666
can't just write software in the rear

00:19:28.666 --> 00:19:30.433
and hand it off to the military

00:19:30.733 --> 00:19:33.133
As one Palantir executive put it:

00:19:33.333 --> 00:19:36.200
you have to be as close as possible to the actual environment

00:19:36.333 --> 00:19:38.466
You need to understand what the constraints are

00:19:38.466 --> 00:19:40.800
rapid iteration and delivery of updates

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:43.500
you need to be continuously developing your software

00:19:43.933 --> 00:19:46.466
The algorithms must be constantly adjusted

00:19:46.466 --> 00:19:48.500
as the battlefield situation evolves and the data updates

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:49.900
And that means

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:52.733
engineers and officers must work closely together every day

00:19:52.733 --> 00:19:55.000
solving concrete operational problems together

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:58.733
This is the core meaning of the Military-Tech Complex

00:19:59.133 --> 00:20:01.200
Not AI fighting on its own

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:03.533
Not machines teaming up with humans

00:20:03.633 --> 00:20:06.266
but a group of civilian experts from tech companies

00:20:06.266 --> 00:20:07.866
alongside a group of professional soldiers

00:20:07.933 --> 00:20:10.866
forming an unprecedented hybrid team

00:20:11.266 --> 00:20:12.833
working in the same command center

00:20:12.833 --> 00:20:14.500
toward the same mission

00:20:15.033 --> 00:20:18.266
Many commentators love to use the phrase "human-machine collaboration"

00:20:18.433 --> 00:20:19.200
as if AI

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.433
were a member of the team—anthropomorphizing it

00:20:22.633 --> 00:20:24.233
But Anthony King says

00:20:24.233 --> 00:20:26.000
that's a misleading framing

00:20:26.233 --> 00:20:27.866
AI doesn't define the mission

00:20:27.866 --> 00:20:29.500
doesn't decide the workflow

00:20:29.733 --> 00:20:32.266
The real collaboration is between people and people

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:35.000
between those in uniform and those in civilian clothes

00:20:35.333 --> 00:20:37.200
AI is just

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:38.833
a very powerful tool in their hands

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:42.533
This is the profound transformation happening below the waterline

00:20:43.033 --> 00:20:44.700
Not machines replacing humans

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:47.900
but a brand-new form of human organization emerging

00:20:48.133 --> 00:20:51.233
Tech companies are no longer just selling products to the military

00:20:51.233 --> 00:20:53.200
no longer just suppliers

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:56.400
They have become part of military operations themselves

00:20:56.866 --> 00:20:57.800
And this transformation

00:20:57.800 --> 00:21:00.466
brought consequences no one anticipated

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:02.833
The first counterintuitive consequence is:

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:05.266
AI hasn't made war faster—

00:21:05.266 --> 00:21:07.700
it's actually slowed war down

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:08.533
Many assumed that warfare in the AI age would be blitzkrieg:

00:21:08.533 --> 00:21:10.700
blitzkrieg

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:11.533
precision strikes

00:21:11.533 --> 00:21:12.466
swift decisive victory

00:21:12.466 --> 00:21:13.433
clean and decisive

00:21:13.666 --> 00:21:15.666
But what the Ukrainian battlefield tells us

00:21:15.666 --> 00:21:17.300
is the exact opposite

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:19.633
The Battle of Bakhmut is the best example

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:21.133
In August 2022

00:21:21.133 --> 00:21:23.833
Russian forces began assaulting the Ukrainian town of Bakhmut

00:21:24.033 --> 00:21:25.800
The fighting lasted a full 8 months

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:26.733
Ukrainian defenders

00:21:26.733 --> 00:21:28.833
turned Bakhmut into a fortress

00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:31.333
while Russian forces fought building by building

00:21:31.666 --> 00:21:35.200
Outside the city both sides dug trenches, bunkers, and fortifications

00:21:35.333 --> 00:21:37.933
Both sides were actually fighting trench warfare

00:21:38.266 --> 00:21:38.833
A scene that

00:21:38.833 --> 00:21:41.666
looked exactly like the trench-filled battlefields of World War One

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:44.433
I remember at the time some military enthusiasts mocked this,

00:21:44.433 --> 00:21:46.300
saying Russia and Ukraine were amateurs

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:48.333
somehow waging a 21st-century war

00:21:48.333 --> 00:21:49.900
at the level of WWI

00:21:50.233 --> 00:21:51.400
In May 2023

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:53.333
when Bakhmut finally fell

00:21:53.333 --> 00:21:55.633
The Economist directly compared the battle

00:21:55.633 --> 00:21:58.700
to the famous Battle of Verdun from WWI

00:21:59.066 --> 00:22:00.866
Although with fewer casualties

00:22:00.866 --> 00:22:02.300
the fighting at Bakhmut

00:22:02.300 --> 00:22:04.500
lasted four months longer than Verdun

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:07.500
But here's a very strange detail:

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:08.066
namely

00:22:08.066 --> 00:22:10.800
while those soldiers were

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:12.400
slaughtering each other in muddy trenches

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:15.400
both sides were making full use of cutting-edge technology—

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:17.933
AI, data, drones, satellites

00:22:18.233 --> 00:22:19.800
Every day over Bakhmut

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:21.866
dozens of drones were in the air

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.900
Both sides were using electronic warfare to jam each other's signals

00:22:24.933 --> 00:22:26.866
Both were coordinating their forces

00:22:26.866 --> 00:22:28.066
through digitized command systems

00:22:28.333 --> 00:22:31.233
This was a WWI-style trench war,

00:22:31.233 --> 00:22:32.100
powered by AI

00:22:32.633 --> 00:22:34.833
So why did this bizarre situation emerge?

00:22:35.233 --> 00:22:36.733
Anthony King's explanation is

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:37.333
precisely because

00:22:37.333 --> 00:22:40.066
both sides could see each other clearly

00:22:40.066 --> 00:22:41.600
through AI and sensor networks

00:22:41.800 --> 00:22:44.700
no one could move without being spotted

00:22:45.066 --> 00:22:46.500
The greater the precision strike capability

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:47.900
the more dangerous mobility becomes

00:22:48.133 --> 00:22:49.700
So the result wasn't blitzkrieg

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:50.800
but stalemate

00:22:51.133 --> 00:22:51.600
Both sides

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:54.300
effectively neutralized each other's ability to maneuver

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:56.233
and the front lines froze

00:22:56.633 --> 00:22:59.533
Each individual strike was indeed faster and more accurate

00:22:59.633 --> 00:23:02.633
but the overall tempo of the war actually slowed down

00:23:02.666 --> 00:23:03.033
So

00:23:03.033 --> 00:23:06.266
those who imagined AI would make war swift and decisive

00:23:06.266 --> 00:23:07.700
had it completely backwards

00:23:07.733 --> 00:23:08.833
Warfare in the AI age

00:23:08.833 --> 00:23:12.000
is more likely to be prolonged, positional, and attritional

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.066
not a rapid resolution through precision strikes

00:23:14.066 --> 00:23:15.600
quickly decisive

00:23:16.133 --> 00:23:16.600
Then

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:19.266
we come to the second unexpected consequence

00:23:19.266 --> 00:23:21.533
of the Military-Tech Complex's entry into warfare

00:23:21.666 --> 00:23:23.933
And this consequence makes many people even more uneasy

00:23:24.333 --> 00:23:24.733
That is:

00:23:24.733 --> 00:23:27.866
when you hand key military capabilities to private companies

00:23:27.866 --> 00:23:30.600
you simultaneously hand over part of your strategic decision-making power

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:31.533
as well

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:32.866
This isn't theory—

00:23:32.866 --> 00:23:34.300
it has already happened

00:23:34.333 --> 00:23:35.300
February 2022

00:23:35.300 --> 00:23:37.033
after Russia invaded Ukraine

00:23:37.033 --> 00:23:38.800
Elon Musk's Starlink satellite system

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:41.000
quickly became Ukraine's lifeline

00:23:41.400 --> 00:23:42.466
Ukraine's Deputy Prime Minister Fedorov

00:23:42.466 --> 00:23:43.733
publicly appealed to Musk on Twitter

00:23:43.733 --> 00:23:45.800
to provide help

00:23:46.066 --> 00:23:46.700
Two days later

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:49.300
500 Starlink terminals were delivered to Ukraine

00:23:49.666 --> 00:23:52.666
Musk also had a video call with Zelensky

00:23:52.733 --> 00:23:53.600
By July

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:56.333
Starlink had already provided 15,000 terminals

00:23:56.666 --> 00:23:58.533
Ukraine's battlefield management system, Delta

00:23:58.533 --> 00:24:00.466
ran entirely on Starlink

00:24:00.866 --> 00:24:02.600
One Ukrainian officer said outright:

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:03.733
without Starlink

00:24:03.733 --> 00:24:05.500
we would lose this war

00:24:06.066 --> 00:24:07.933
But in September 2022

00:24:07.933 --> 00:24:10.100
things took a dramatic turn

00:24:10.633 --> 00:24:13.400
According to Isaacson's biography of Musk

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:14.866
Ukraine was planning

00:24:14.866 --> 00:24:17.066
to use unmanned surface vessels packed with explosives

00:24:17.066 --> 00:24:19.133
to attack Russian warships in Sevastopol

00:24:19.133 --> 00:24:20.233
launching a raid

00:24:20.666 --> 00:24:22.466
Russia's ambassador to the U.S., Antonov

00:24:22.466 --> 00:24:24.133
privately warned Musk

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:25.700
that attacking Crimea was a red line

00:24:25.700 --> 00:24:27.133
and could trigger nuclear war

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:29.433
Musk was alarmed by this prospect

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:32.433
He quickly consulted Biden's National Security Advisor Sullivan

00:24:32.433 --> 00:24:34.933
and Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff Milley

00:24:34.933 --> 00:24:36.466
to discuss the issue

00:24:36.866 --> 00:24:38.633
He also assured Antonov

00:24:38.633 --> 00:24:40.600
that Starlink would only be used for defensive purposes

00:24:41.333 --> 00:24:41.866
But ultimately

00:24:41.866 --> 00:24:43.033
Musk decided

00:24:43.033 --> 00:24:43.600
to cut off

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:46.600
Ukraine's Starlink access in the direction of Crimea

00:24:46.866 --> 00:24:50.600
Ukraine's planned raid immediately collapsed

00:24:50.666 --> 00:24:53.233
Musk even messaged Fedorov directly

00:24:53.233 --> 00:24:55.600
advising him: while you still have the upper hand

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:56.800
seek peace

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:00.100
This shook the Pentagon to its core

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:02.866
Secretary of the Air Force Kendall publicly criticized:

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:05.466
if we're going to rely on commercial systems in combat

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:08.033
we must have assurances of their availability

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:08.733
Otherwise

00:25:08.733 --> 00:25:11.433
they're a convenience and a cost-saver in peacetime

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:12.300
but in wartime

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:15.066
they are not something we can depend on

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:16.033
But the problem is

00:25:16.033 --> 00:25:17.033
these tech companies

00:25:17.033 --> 00:25:19.133
are not agents of the state

00:25:19.266 --> 00:25:20.833
They are independent actors

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:21.500
Musk

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:24.500
had no obligation to support Ukraine's war effort

00:25:24.833 --> 00:25:26.733
But once he chose to provide support

00:25:26.733 --> 00:25:28.900
he inevitably acquired the ability

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:30.000
to influence policy

00:25:30.533 --> 00:25:33.100
In the dispute over the Crimea raid

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:34.433
Musk was in direct communication with

00:25:34.433 --> 00:25:35.000
Ukraine,

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:37.666
and Russia's top political and military leaders

00:25:37.666 --> 00:25:39.033
all at once

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:40.866
Like it or not

00:25:40.866 --> 00:25:43.533
Zelensky, Biden, Milley, Antonov

00:25:43.533 --> 00:25:46.633
all had to acknowledge Musk's power and influence

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:48.333
Anthony King says

00:25:48.333 --> 00:25:50.700
AI hasn't automated strategy

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:52.833
the way many feared

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:55.133
But it has done something equally profound:

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:57.733
it has politicized strategy

00:25:57.733 --> 00:25:58.833
Private tech companies

00:25:58.833 --> 00:26:01.333
are now players in the strategic game

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:03.700
The rise of the Military-Tech Complex

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:06.400
is in essence a kind of

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:07.700
quasi-privatization of national defense strategy

00:26:08.066 --> 00:26:09.300
National defense strategy

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:12.533
is no longer solely determined by the state

00:26:12.933 --> 00:26:14.033
Anthony King says

00:26:14.033 --> 00:26:17.133
this is a situation unseen

00:26:17.133 --> 00:26:18.900
since the era of the East India Company in the 19th century

00:26:19.466 --> 00:26:20.933
This is how AI is changing warfare in the real world

00:26:20.933 --> 00:26:22.933
This is how AI is changing warfare in the real world

00:26:23.466 --> 00:26:25.866
Finally, let's step from reality back into imagination

00:26:26.033 --> 00:26:28.033
and return to the opening image

00:26:28.133 --> 00:26:30.066
Why does the author argue in this book

00:26:30.066 --> 00:26:32.833
that Terminator-style AI warfare is an illusion?

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:34.666
To answer this question

00:26:34.666 --> 00:26:36.266
we first need to understand

00:26:36.266 --> 00:26:38.533
where the capability boundaries of this generation of AI lie

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:39.933
Today's AI

00:26:39.933 --> 00:26:41.800
whether ChatGPT or AlphaGo

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:43.033
or military algorithms

00:26:43.100 --> 00:26:44.900
is all built on the same underlying logic:

00:26:45.066 --> 00:26:47.666
training models on massive amounts of data

00:26:47.666 --> 00:26:50.133
and letting models learn to identify patterns in that data

00:26:50.433 --> 00:26:53.433
When the training data is large enough and reliable enough

00:26:53.433 --> 00:26:54.700
AI within the domain of problems

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:56.633
that data covers

00:26:56.633 --> 00:26:59.500
will exhibit pattern recognition capabilities far beyond human capacity

00:26:59.866 --> 00:27:02.100
AlphaGo defeating the world Go champion

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:04.666
AI predicting protein folding structures

00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:06.700
AI identifying cancer in medical images—

00:27:06.866 --> 00:27:08.466
these achievements are all real

00:27:08.466 --> 00:27:09.800
and all for the same reason:

00:27:09.933 --> 00:27:12.133
highly reliable training data

00:27:12.133 --> 00:27:14.300
and a relatively stable problem structure

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:16.900
But there's another side to this coin:

00:27:17.233 --> 00:27:18.400
once the real environment

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:20.900
exceeds the scope of the training data

00:27:20.933 --> 00:27:21.233
then

00:27:21.233 --> 00:27:24.200
AI's judgments can go badly wrong

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:26.833
and it doesn't even know it's wrong

00:27:26.833 --> 00:27:29.200
And war is precisely the scenario

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:31.600
where real environments most easily diverge from training data

00:27:32.066 --> 00:27:33.600
Battlefields change in an instant—

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:34.500
every engagement

00:27:34.500 --> 00:27:35.433
may present

00:27:35.433 --> 00:27:37.600
situations the model has never encountered

00:27:37.833 --> 00:27:40.100
And the enemy will actively deceive you—

00:27:40.133 --> 00:27:41.900
they'll disguise the positions of their forces

00:27:41.900 --> 00:27:43.300
broadcast false signals

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:45.266
and pollute your data sources

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:48.100
Models can of course update and self-correct in real time

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:51.100
but war does not permit trial and error

00:27:51.466 --> 00:27:54.000
If a recommendation algorithm goes wrong

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:56.633
the worst case is it recommends a book you don't want to read

00:27:56.833 --> 00:27:59.466
But if the same kind of error occurs on a battlefield

00:27:59.466 --> 00:28:00.333
it might mean

00:28:00.333 --> 00:28:03.033
a bomb landing on a civilian apartment building

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:05.633
So no responsible decision-maker

00:28:05.633 --> 00:28:07.633
would hand over life-or-death decisions

00:28:07.633 --> 00:28:08.233
to a system

00:28:08.233 --> 00:28:11.066
that could go wrong at any moment due to environmental changes

00:28:11.066 --> 00:28:13.500
and wouldn't even know it had erred

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:17.066
In many situations AI is a top-tier intelligence officer

00:28:17.066 --> 00:28:19.133
but it is absolutely not a decision-maker

00:28:19.533 --> 00:28:21.500
And the problem doesn't stop there

00:28:21.866 --> 00:28:23.300
Even if we assume that someday

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:25.800
AI can overcome data quality issues

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:29.000
military decision-making is far more than data processing

00:28:29.333 --> 00:28:31.333
What does a battlefield commander need to do?

00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:34.200
They must understand the objectives set by political leaders

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:37.066
and judge which actions are politically acceptable

00:28:37.333 --> 00:28:39.200
They must negotiate and coordinate with allies

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:40.866
persuade, reassure

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:42.733
and sometimes even overrule

00:28:42.733 --> 00:28:43.666
subordinates who hold opposing views

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:45.733
They must read the enemy's intentions—

00:28:45.933 --> 00:28:48.100
not as probabilities calculated from data

00:28:48.100 --> 00:28:50.433
but as a judgment about a living, breathing opponent

00:28:50.433 --> 00:28:52.133
with ambitions and fears

00:28:52.466 --> 00:28:54.500
They must weigh the moral cost of an operation

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:55.700
and its cultural ramifications

00:28:55.900 --> 00:28:58.100
and motivate their soldiers to fight

00:28:58.100 --> 00:28:59.333
to face death

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:01.466
These require leadership

00:29:01.466 --> 00:29:02.666
not processing power

00:29:03.233 --> 00:29:04.233
This generation of AI

00:29:04.233 --> 00:29:06.100
can't even be trusted with tactical-level decision-making

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:07.666
let alone hand it over

00:29:07.866 --> 00:29:09.433
let alone decisions of a difficulty

00:29:09.433 --> 00:29:10.133
orders of magnitude greater—

00:29:10.133 --> 00:29:12.700
strategic decisions

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:14.233
and political decisions

00:29:14.466 --> 00:29:15.100
At that level

00:29:15.100 --> 00:29:17.433
today's AI hasn't even come close

00:29:18.033 --> 00:29:19.033
For the military, this situation

00:29:19.033 --> 00:29:21.500
is well understood internally

00:29:21.933 --> 00:29:23.133
Anthony King carefully reviewed

00:29:23.133 --> 00:29:24.733
the U.S.

00:29:24.733 --> 00:29:25.900
UK and NATO

00:29:25.900 --> 00:29:28.800
AI military strategy documents published in recent years

00:29:29.033 --> 00:29:29.633
These documents

00:29:29.633 --> 00:29:31.866
determine real budget allocations

00:29:31.866 --> 00:29:34.866
and the direction of military development for the next decade or so

00:29:35.133 --> 00:29:36.700
Money is more honest than anything

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:38.666
The results are very telling:

00:29:38.800 --> 00:29:40.700
in all these documents determining technology direction

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:42.000
and budgets

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:44.666
AI's primary role is never autonomous combat

00:29:44.666 --> 00:29:46.033
but data processing

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:46.900
The U.S.

00:29:46.900 --> 00:29:50.400
2021 National Security Commission on AI report

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:51.733
is very explicit on this

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:52.900
The report states:

00:29:52.900 --> 00:29:55.133
AI will revolutionize intelligence work—

00:29:55.366 --> 00:29:57.133
machines will sift through massive data

00:29:57.133 --> 00:29:58.666
locate key information

00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:00.666
fuse datasets from different sources

00:30:00.733 --> 00:30:03.266
to support analysts and decision-makers

00:30:03.533 --> 00:30:05.300
The UK and Israel's AI strategies

00:30:05.300 --> 00:30:06.666
follow the same logic

00:30:07.266 --> 00:30:09.466
So back to our opening question:

00:30:09.666 --> 00:30:12.100
how is AI actually changing warfare?

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:13.933
The answer is not the Terminator

00:30:13.933 --> 00:30:14.933
not Skynet

00:30:14.933 --> 00:30:16.200
but the All-Seeing Eye

00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:18.800
AI has launched an intelligence revolution

00:30:19.133 --> 00:30:21.100
It can devour massive data

00:30:21.100 --> 00:30:23.800
aggregating intelligence at speeds impossible for humans

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:25.000
locking onto targets

00:30:25.233 --> 00:30:26.666
The All-Seeing Eye is already profoundly

00:30:26.666 --> 00:30:28.466
reshaping the face of warfare

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:31.133
But the All-Seeing Eye doesn't operate out of thin air

00:30:31.333 --> 00:30:32.666
To make it work

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:35.600
Silicon Valley engineers moved into operational command centers

00:30:35.666 --> 00:30:38.466
Tech companies became participants in military operations

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:40.833
This Military-Tech Complex

00:30:40.833 --> 00:30:44.066
is profoundly reshaping the power structure on the battlefield

00:30:44.633 --> 00:30:47.700
So: will AI replace humans in waging war?

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:49.800
That's entirely the wrong question

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:53.300
The real question worth asking about AI's All-Seeing Eye is:

00:30:53.300 --> 00:30:54.800
who has the right to use it?

00:30:54.800 --> 00:30:56.400
who can shut it off?

00:30:56.933 --> 00:30:58.433
AI hasn't replaced humans

00:30:58.466 --> 00:31:00.900
but the power relationships between humans

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:02.666
are being redefined by it

00:31:03.433 --> 00:31:05.600
And that's a wrap on *AI, Automation and War*

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:06.900
for today

00:31:07.033 --> 00:31:08.233
If you enjoyed today's episode

00:31:08.233 --> 00:31:10.433
please give it a like, share it, and subscribe to the channel

00:31:10.433 --> 00:31:12.000
That's the greatest support you can give me

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:12.700
I'm Wei Zhichao

00:31:12.700 --> 00:31:14.233
See you in the next book

